package net.portswigger.mcp.tools

/** Shared published planning guidance; no new execution mode, branch or permission gate. */
internal const val LOGIN_PREPARATION_GUIDANCE = """

Login preparation and branch selection:
Preserve successful prerequisite login -> prerequisite logout -> diagnostic login -> post-request logout. Prerequisite Macros use the observed known-good credentials. The benign diagnostic login used for preparation also uses known-good credentials; the later Scanner request retains its intended attack inputs. Update only necessary CSRF/session values, never replace diagnostic username/password payloads with prerequisite credentials. Carry each login's own newly issued SID into its corresponding logout and verify invalidation of that same SID. Return the diagnostic response with current_response.
Choose necessary scenarios before applying the repetition count. Five repetitions mean five complete checks of the selected workflow, not five failed logins. For login preparation the default is requiredBranches=["normal"] with branchSetup={}. Do not automatically add invalid-password, wrong-user or credential-guessing branches to satisfy the five-check rule or to manufacture a no-session response for CPH. A separate bad-credential preparation test is not implied by an ActiveScan request; include it only when the user's requested test actually requires it and account/IP/time-window effects have been assessed. Previously observed failure evidence may be reviewed with its configuration and limits stated; do not relabel it as a new live test.
The valid/no-recovery and invalid/recovery branch requirement applies to ordinary authenticated business workflows using validationDecision=USE. Invalid means a missing/expired owned session while recovery still uses known-good credentials; it does not mean a wrong password and does not impose these branches on a login diagnostic using OMIT for isolation.
If normal authentication is temporarily refused, preserve the response, wait according to observed application behavior, and re-check normal authentication. Do not automatically weaken the target's protections, reset counters, or keep sending failed credentials. Normal prerequisite credentials do not imply excluding the login password field from the diagnostic scan. Do not alter attack selectors to compensate for an unnecessary preparation test; separate that scope decision from Macro verification.
For HTTP 429, inspect automatic-throttling settings, the actual Task pool assignment and the existing sequence before deciding to stop or declaring the entire Scan failed. Automatic throttling adds delay; it does not guarantee that the failed prerequisite or diagnostic check is retried. A later successful-looking response does not validate the earlier affected interval. Distinguish rejected known-good prerequisites from expected rejection of deliberately attacked CSRF/session input, and retain unresolved checks for review. Re-checking normal authentication requires the existing user authorization; this guidance does not automatically send or retry requests.
"""
